    "text": "A&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mi>Li</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mn>7</mn></mmultiscripts></math> target is bombarded with a proton beam current of magnitude&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> for an hour to produce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mi>Be</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mn>7</mn></mmultiscripts></math>&nbsp;of activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>disintegrations per second. If we assume that one radioactive nucleus of beryllium is produced by bombarding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn></math>&nbsp;protons, then its half-life is close to",
